  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nSummer heat will build back over the weekend as an upper level ridge \nmoves in from western CONUS. As it stands, guidance is currently \nsuggesting H850 temps in the 20 - 30C with surface highs cresting \nthe mid 90s on Saturday with surface highs reaching even higher to \ntriple digits in the eastern Panhandle on Sunday. These warmer \ntemperatures along with afternoon humidity values dropping to \n20 - 25 percent across the eastern Panhandle into the Sandhills,\nwith moderate probability (30 - 50%) of humidity dropping into \nthe teens, especially Sunday. Combine this with gusty south \nsoutheast winds and little to no wetting precipitation expected,\nwill make for the likely return of elevated to near critical \nfire weather concerns for the western half of our CWA. Will \ncontinue to closely monitor the forecast for any increased \nconfidence with this trend. \n\nBy Monday afternoon, the ridge will begin to breakdown as an upper \nlevel trough begins to dig into the region from western Canada. This \nwill largely be the driving force for stronger winds and heightened \nfire weather concerns. LREF guidance suggests at 50 - 70% \nprobability of wind gusts in excess of 30 mph gusts across the \nregion. With highs in the mid to upper 90s and afternoon \nhumidity will drop to low 20s to upper teens along and west of \nHighway 83, this will make for near critical fire weather \nconditions, where if the trend holds, could warrant some fire \nweather headlines, depending on how receptive fuels are to \nrecent rains. This will be another trend to monitor for any \nchanges. A cool front will push through Monday night, returning \nto near seasonal high temperatures for the rest of the week. \nThere is some signal for better precipitation chances Monday \nnight with a cool front moving in but, there remains some \nensemble disagreement over just how much precipitation will fall\nduring this time with GEFS and ECMWF members showing 0 - 0.1\" \nwhile GFS being on the aggressive side with up to a half inch of\nrain by the end of Wednesday.",
